infographic compare-binary-horizontal-underline-text-vs
data
  title Boolean flags vs one literal tag: the state space
  desc The type allows eight combinations but only three are real states — the tag closes the gap
  compares
    - label Boolean flags { loading; error?; data? }
      children
        - label 8 representable combinations
          desc 2^3 from loading x error? x data? — most are nonsense
        - label Only 3 are real states
          desc loading, error, ok
        - label 5 illegal states allowed
          desc e.g. data set while error is set — the crash from the Hook
        - label No exhaustiveness check
          desc Nothing forces a component to handle every state
    - label Discriminated union (one literal tag)
      children
        - label 3 representable states
          desc tag kind = "ok" | "error" | "loading"
        - label All 3 are valid
          desc Each variant's fields are reachable only in its branch
        - label 0 illegal states
          desc Illegal combinations are unrepresentable by construction
        - label Exhaustiveness via assertNever
          desc A new variant is a compile error at every unhandled switch
theme
  palette #f43f5e #14b8a6
